  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(-1,4).$\n\nIII. It's an increasing function",
  "worked_solution": "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194784/assets/5d97ca13c1289558c6bb8e8ce50fb1ef5745f54acf27346a32d6451b1bb7f9bb.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x,$ so the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(-1)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-1}=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true.",
